\pard\f0\fs24 the language is \b tcl\f1  \b0 (very minimal variation)\f0\par
\par
the path and folders are like linux? "\b /path/to/object\b0 "\par
\par
`\b sel\b0 ` is like cd\par
so:\par
`\b sel /sys/ \b0 ` and then `\b sel ..\b0 ` equals `\b sel /\b0 `\par
\par
\b dir \b0 gives objects in this path (not commands)\par
\par
\b psel \b0 gives current path\par
\par
`\b set x y\b0 ` is like `\b x=y\b0 `. to use other command output `\b set x [cmd]\b0 `\f1  like `\b set path [psel]\b0 `\f0\par
\par
`\b puts "\f1 a b c\f0 "\b0 ` prints \ul\f1 a b c\f0  \ulnone to console (therefore, to the .log file)\par
`\b puts $var\b0 ` prints variable "var"\par
\par
\b global x\b0  -> access the global variable x. (for functions => proc)\par
\par
Some important places:\par
\b /usr/level/entities\b0  -> see all objects in loaded level, while playing. Not good to change in runtime.. but good to verify.\par
\par
\b /sys/servers/gameserver/player\b0  -> player object, that has info about the main fps player (position, rotation etc)\par
\par
\b getcmds \b0 -> get available commands in this path/object. syntax is \par
\highlight1\{return type\}_name_\{input type\}\highlight0 :\par

